  "assumptions": [
    "This is the exact combinatorial upper-bound case for the same 15-day synthetic holdout used by the dynamic and static comparators; it is not operator traffic.",
    "Days 0–14 of the source 30-day synthetic trace form the training window, and days 15–29 form the holdout. No holdout sample is used to choose a fixed assignment.",
    "At each held-out interval the oracle enumerates every feasible pair of two cells and maximizes sum(priority_weight × min(cell critical excess, 10 Mbps)).",
    "The 10 Mbps per-beam cap and 2-beam budget are identical to the static and dynamic comparators.",
    "Ties in objective value are resolved by the lexicographically ascending sorted cell-ID pair.",
    "Raw critical-excess throughput and priority-weighted critical-excess service are both reported because optimizing one can reduce the other; do not collapse the Pareto trade into one unqualified leaderboard score.",
    "This exact oracle is an allocation upper bound, not a deployable online policy: it consumes the current interval's offered-load row to determine the best allocation and assumes no forecast error or decision latency.",
    "No radio propagation, SINR, interference, positioning, wind, backhaul, edge compute, energy, flight or network reliability model is included.",
    "This is a synthetic oracle baseline, not a reproduction or beating of the Mbarek et al. 2026 real-operator-trace result."
  ]
